var lunrIndex = [{"id":"changelog","title":"Changelog","body":"All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\n\nThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https:\/\/keepachangelog.com\/en\/1.0.0\/),\nand this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https:\/\/semver.org\/spec\/v2.0.0.html).\n\n## [Unreleased]\n\n## [10.2.2] - 2026-10-21\n\n### Changed\n\n- **Breaking:** the documented directory for Unit and Integration tests is now `tests\/src\/Unit\/` and `tests\/src\/Integration\/`, not `tests\/Unit\/` and `tests\/Integration\/`. The `src\/` segment matters: Drupal core's own test runner (`vendor\/bin\/phpunit -c web\/core`, drupal.org's GitLab CI) discovers `tests\/src\/Unit\/` automatically, with no bootstrap and no trace of this package, the same way it already discovers Kernel and Functional tests \u2014 the old path was invisible to it for no technical reason. Existing projects should move their `tests\/Unit\/` and `tests\/Integration\/` directories (and the matching `autoload-dev` PSR-4 path and `` entries in `phpunit.xml`) to `tests\/src\/Unit\/` and `tests\/src\/Integration\/`.\n- _init\/run-phpunit-tests.sh_ now flushes the autoload map cache automatically when a module is added, removed, moved or re-symlinked, instead of failing with `Class \"...\" not found` until `--flush` is run. It compares directory mtimes to the cache file using bash builtins only (no added run time), so only _modules_, _themes_, _profiles_ and their direct subdirectories (e.g. _contrib_, _custom_) are watched; a module nested deeper still needs `--flush`. Copy the new block into your copy of the runner.\n\n## [10.2.1] - 2026-10-04\n\n### Added\n\n- `DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_VERSION='^9'` now installs the 9.x line.\n- The install path and the Drupal core path are configurable with `DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_INSTALL_PATH` and `DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_DRUPAL_CORE`; the generated `phpunit.xml` and runner follow them.\n\n### Changed\n\n- **Breaking:** install with `curl -sSL https:\/\/raw.githubusercontent.com\/aklump\/drupal-phpunit-integration\/main\/bin\/install.php | php` (was `install.sh | bash`).\n- All installer logic now lives in `bin\/install.php`, which bootstraps `tests_phpunit\/`, requires the package and configures it. `bin\/install.sh` is removed.\n- PHPUnit 11 is now configured the same way as PHPUnit 10.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The installer resolved the default `@dev` version to the `9.x-dev` branch, which has no `bin\/install.php`, and then failed without making the failure obvious. The default is now `dev-main`.\n- The installer now stops with a prominent failure banner and fix instructions on every error.\n- The installer wrote literal `\\n` characters into `tests_phpunit\/.gitignore` and called an undefined `error_exit`.\n- The installer exited with \"Runner script not found\" at its last step because it looked for `init\/run_*.sh` and the runner is `init\/run-phpunit-tests.sh`.\n\n## [10.0.0] - 2025-11-27\n\n### Changed\n\n- Bumped version to match the PhpUnit version supported, e.g. 10.\n- Simplified the installation process considerably\n\n## [9.0.0] - 2025-11-27\n\n### Changed\n\n- Bumped version to match the PhpUnit version supported, e.g. 9.\n\n## [0.0.23] - 2025-10-10\n\n### Added\n\n- Ability to use `-c` on CLI to point to a different config file.\n- Support for `getTitle` method when creating entity mocks using \\Drupal\\node\\NodeInterface.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- Ability to run self tests with _self.xml_ config file.\n\n## [0.0.21] - 2025-03-27\n\n### Added\n\n- Better handling of environment variables\n\n### Changed\n\n- Language change \"integration_tests\" to \"phpunit_tests\" in several places\n\n## [0.0.18] - 2024-04-27\n\n### Changed\n\n- autoload-dev is now only scanned during --flush\n\n### Fixed\n\n- An issue with some autoload-dev paths not being found.\n\n## [0.0.15] - 2024-02-14\n\n### Changed\n\n- init\/run-phpunit-tests.sh; you should cherry pick these changes on update.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- An issue that wouldn't pass more than one arg to PhpUnit in file: init\/run-phpunit-tests.sh was\n\n## [0.0.1] - 2023-10-28\n\n### Added\n\n- lorem\n\n### Changed\n\n- Namespace changed from `\\AKlump\\Drupal\\PHPUnit\\Integration\\` to `AKlump\\Drupal\\PHPUnit\\Integration\\`\n\n### Deprecated\n\n- lorem\n\n### Removed\n\n- lorem\n\n### Fixed\n\n- lorem\n\n### Security\n\n- lorem"},{"id":"composer_autoload_dev","title":"Composer Autoload Dev","body":"The _composer.json_ property `autoload-dev` is not be loaded from the packages by default by Composer because it's a root-only key, however it's best practice\n(https:\/\/getcomposer.org\/doc\/04-schema.md#autoload-dev) to use autoload-dev\nfor test-only classes.\n\nTherefore this project makes allowance by scanning for autoload-dev namespaces inside the tests directories included in _phpunit.xml_. And passing those off to the autoloader automatically.\n\nThis occurs during `--flush`.\n\n@see `\\AKlump\\Drupal\\PHPUnit\\Integration\\Runner\\AutoloadDev` for more info."},{"id":"developers","title":"Developer Notes","body":"### How to Run Package Self Tests\n\nIn order to self-test this package you must be able to point to a Drupal codebase using the variables shown below.  Also note, you must be using the same PHP version as the codebase.  The tests will use these variables to access the Drupal classes and bootstrapping necessary.\n\nThe PHPUnit configuration file _self.xml_, is located in the root of the package.\n\n```shell\nexport VENDOR_PATH=\/Users\/aklump\/Code\/Projects\/InTheLoftStudios\/TabulonSB\/site\/app\/vendor\nexport DRUPAL_ROOT=\/Users\/aklump\/Code\/Projects\/InTheLoftStudios\/TabulonSB\/site\/app\/web\nexport INSTALL_PATH=\/Users\/aklump\/Code\/Projects\/InTheLoftStudios\/TabulonSB\/site\/app\/tests_phpunit\/\nvendor\/bin\/phpunit -c \/Users\/aklump\/Code\/Packages\/php\/drupal-phpunit-integration\/app\/self.xml\n\n```"},{"id":"readme","title":"drupal-phpunit-integration","body":"> PHPUnit tests for Drupal custom code that needs Drupal classes, without writing a full kernel test.\n\n![Hero image](..\/..\/images\/drupal-phpunit-integration.jpg)\n\n## Summary\n\nDrupal's own test types sit at two extremes: `UnitTestCase` still pulls in Drupal's test base classes, and kernel and functional tests need a database and a bootstrapped site. Much custom module code falls in between: it uses Drupal interfaces and classes, but its logic can be tested with plain PHPUnit and a few mocks. This package gives that code a home. It installs a separate PHPUnit project in `tests_phpunit\/` next to your Drupal site, autoloads Drupal core and your modules' `autoload-dev` namespaces from a cache that is built once and reused, and gives you a runner script, `bin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh`, that points PHPUnit at the right configuration from anywhere in the project. When your tests do need Drupal's database, it fills in `SIMPLETEST_DB` and `SIMPLETEST_BASE_URL` for you from your environment, Lando or Drush.\n\n## Quick Start\n\nFrom the root of a Composer-based Drupal project (the directory that contains `web\/core`):\n\n```shell\ncurl -sSL https:\/\/raw.githubusercontent.com\/aklump\/drupal-phpunit-integration\/main\/bin\/install.php | php\n```\n\nThe installer creates `tests_phpunit\/`, requires this package into it, adds `drupal\/core-dev` to your project if it is missing, copies Drupal core's `phpunit.xml.dist` to `tests_phpunit\/phpunit.xml` and copies the runner to `bin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh`. It ends with:\n\n```text\n## Final step\nRun the tests with:\nbin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh --flush\n\nInstallation completed successfully!\n```\n\nPoint the test suite at your module (see [Configuration](#configuration)), then run your tests. Use `--flush` the first time so the autoload cache is built:\n\n```shell\nbin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh --flush\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\n- A Composer-based Drupal site whose webroot is `web\/`; the installer stops with a failure banner (`Drupal not found at web\/core`) otherwise, unless you set `DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_DRUPAL_CORE`.\n- `drupal\/core-dev` at the same major and minor version as `drupal\/core`. The installer tries to add it for you.\n- Composer, and a PHP version supported by PHPUnit 10.5 or 11, which this package requires. For PHPUnit 9, install the 9.x line (see below).\n\n## Installation\n\n### Installer script\n\nThe Quick Start command is the supported install. It refuses to run if the install path already exists. Three settings are optional environment variables. Set them on the `php` side of the pipe, not before `curl`:\n\n| Variable | Default | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_VERSION` | `dev-main` | The Composer constraint for this package, e.g. `^9` for PHPUnit 9 |\n| `DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_INSTALL_PATH` | `tests_phpunit` | Where to install; absolute, or relative to the directory you run it from |\n| `DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_DRUPAL_CORE` | `web\/core` | Where Drupal core is; absolute, or relative to the directory you run it from |\n\nFor example, to install the 9.x line into `qa\/phpunit` on a site whose webroot is `docroot\/`:\n\n```shell\ncurl -sSL https:\/\/raw.githubusercontent.com\/aklump\/drupal-phpunit-integration\/main\/bin\/install.php \\\n  | DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_VERSION='^9' \\\n    DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_INSTALL_PATH=qa\/phpunit \\\n    DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_DRUPAL_CORE=docroot\/core \\\n    php\n```\n\nThe generated `phpunit.xml` and `bin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh` are pointed at the locations you choose. The rest of this documentation assumes the defaults.\n\n### Installing 9.x (PHPUnit 9)\n\nSet `DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_VERSION` to the 9.x line, as in the example above. The installer always runs the current `bin\/install.php` from `main`, so the result is the same as a normal install.\n\n```shell\ncurl -sSL https:\/\/raw.githubusercontent.com\/aklump\/drupal-phpunit-integration\/main\/bin\/install.php \\\n  | DRUPAL_PHPUNIT_INTEGRATION_VERSION='^9' \\\n    php\n```\n\n### Should `tests_phpunit\/composer.lock` be committed?\n\nThat is up to you, but committing it keeps your test dependencies stable when they are reinstalled.\n\n### Updating\n\n```bash\ncd tests_phpunit\ncomposer update\n```\n\nThis only updates `tests_phpunit\/vendor\/`, so your `phpunit.xml`, runner and support classes are not touched. The changelog notes when the runner template or `phpunit.xml` changes; compare yours with the package's copies from time to time and cherry-pick what you need:\n\n```bash\ncd tests_phpunit\ndiff vendor\/aklump\/drupal-phpunit-integration\/init\/run-phpunit-tests.sh ..\/bin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh\ndiff ..\/web\/core\/phpunit.xml.dist phpunit.xml\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\n### Test suites and source paths\n\nThe installer writes placeholder paths for a module named `my_module` into `tests_phpunit\/phpunit.xml`. Open it and replace the `` directories and the `` directory with your real paths, relative to `tests_phpunit\/`. It is convenient to list your modules' unit tests here as well, so both kinds run together:\n\n```xml\n\n        ..\/web\/modules\/custom\/alpha\/tests\/src\/Unit\/\n\n        ..\/web\/modules\/custom\/alpha\/tests\/src\/Integration\/\n\n```\n\nUse `tests\/src\/Unit\/` and `tests\/src\/Integration\/`, not `tests\/Unit\/`\/`tests\/Integration\/` \u2014 the extra `src\/` segment is required for `tests\/src\/Unit\/` to also be discovered by Drupal core's own conventional test runner (`vendor\/bin\/phpunit -c web\/core`, drupal.org's GitLab CI) with zero configuration, the same way a Kernel or Functional test already is. See [The Conventional Way, Then This Package's Way](@conventional_vs_this_way) for why that one path segment matters.\n\n### The runner\n\n`bin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh` has a configuration block at the top: `INSTALL_PATH`, `DRUPAL_ROOT` and `VENDOR_PATH` (relative to the script), `CODE_COVERAGE` to always generate coverage, `PHPUNIT_ARGS` for default PHPUnit arguments, and an optional `PHP_BIN` directory to put first on `PATH`.\n\n### Database and base URL\n\nKernel and functional tests need `SIMPLETEST_DB`. The `DynamicConfig` extension sets it at bootstrap, using the first of these that has a value:\n\n1. `SIMPLETEST_DB`, if you already set it in `phpunit.xml` or your environment.\n2. `DATABASE_URL__`, where `` is the upper-cased name of the git branch checked out in the Drupal root.\n3. `DATABASE_URL`.\n4. The database of the current Lando app, using its internal connection inside Lando and its external connection from the host.\n5. The output of `drush sql:connect`.\n\n`SIMPLETEST_BASE_URL` works the same way: your own value first, then Lando, then Drush's site URI. `BROWSERTEST_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` defaults to `tests_phpunit\/test_output\/`. If no database can be found, the run stops with instructions for setting `SIMPLETEST_DB`, for example `mysql:\/\/username:password@localhost\/databasename`.\n\n## Usage\n\n### Running tests\n\n```shell\n# Everything in phpunit.xml\nbin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh\n\n# Rebuild the autoload cache first, e.g. after adding a module or namespace\nbin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh --flush\n\n# One test class\nbin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh --filter=FooTest\n\n# One file; paths relative to the project root work\nbin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh web\/modules\/custom\/alpha\/tests\/src\/Integration\/FooTest.php\n\n# HTML coverage report in tests_phpunit\/reports (needs Xdebug or PCOV)\nbin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh --coverage\n```\n\nAny argument the runner does not handle is passed to PHPUnit. `-c` and `--configuration` are ignored on purpose: the runner always uses `tests_phpunit\/phpunit.xml`, so relative paths resolve the same way every time. The autoload cache is built on the first run and reused after that; `autoload-dev` paths are only scanned during `--flush`, so flush when a class is reported as not found.\n\n### Unit tests versus integration tests\n\n```\nweb\/modules\/custom\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 alpha\n    \u251c\u2500\u2500 src\n    \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 Foo.php\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 tests\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500 src\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500 Integration\n            \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 FooTest.php\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500 Unit\n                \u2514\u2500\u2500 FooTest.php\n```\n\nBoth live under `tests\/src\/`, the same root Drupal core itself uses for `Kernel`\/`Functional` \u2014 never under a bare `tests\/Unit\/` or `tests\/Integration\/`, which core's own test runner would never look inside. `tests\/src\/Unit\/FooTest.php` has no Drupal dependencies; it is plain PHPUnit and can run on its own, including through `vendor\/bin\/phpunit -c web\/core --testsuite=unit` with no trace of this package at all. `tests\/src\/Integration\/FooTest.php` uses Drupal classes, so it has to run through `bin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh`, which loads them; \"integration\" isn't a suite core defines, so this one only ever runs through this package regardless of its directory. Use the `Drupal\\Tests\\alpha\\Unit` and `Drupal\\Tests\\alpha\\Integration` namespaces:\n\n```php\nnamespace Drupal\\Tests\\alpha\\Integration;\n\nclass FooTest extends \\PHPUnit\\Framework\\TestCase {\n```\n\nand declare them in the module's `composer.json` so the runner can autoload them:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"autoload\": {\n    \"psr-4\": {\n      \"Drupal\\\\alpha\\\\\": \"src\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"autoload-dev\": {\n    \"psr-4\": {\n      \"Drupal\\\\Tests\\\\alpha\\\\\": \".\/tests\/src\/\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Mocking entities\n\n`MockDrupalEntityTrait` builds entity mocks whose fields behave like the real thing. Fields are keyed by name; each holds a list of items, and a scalar value sets the entity ID:\n\n```php\nuse AKlump\\Drupal\\PHPUnit\\Integration\\Framework\\MockObject\\MockDrupalEntityTrait;\nuse Drupal\\node\\NodeInterface;\n\nfinal class FooTest extends \\PHPUnit\\Framework\\TestCase {\n\n  use MockDrupalEntityTrait;\n\n  public function testTitle() {\n    $node = $this->createEntityMock('node', 'page', [\n      'nid' => 9,\n      'title' => [['value' => 'Somewhere Over the Rainbow']],\n    ], '', NodeInterface::class);\n\n    $this->assertSame('9', $node->id());\n    $this->assertSame('Somewhere Over the Rainbow', $node->getTitle());\n    $this->assertSame('Somewhere Over the Rainbow', $node->get('title')->first()->value);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAn item can hold another mocked entity (`'field_account' => [$account]`), which is then reachable through `->get('entity')->getTarget()->getEntity()`. `createUserMock($fields)` is a shortcut for a mocked `UserInterface`. To make any mock iterable, use `MakeMockIterable`:\n\n```php\n(new MakeMockIterable())($mock, ['lorem', 'ipsum']);\n```\n\n### Your own test support classes\n\n`tests_phpunit\/src\/` is autoloaded under the `AKlump\\Drupal\\PHPUnit\\Integration` namespace. Put shared traits and helpers there.\n\n## License\n\n[GPL-2.0](..\/..\/..\/LICENSE)"},{"id":"troubleshooting","title":"Error: Drupal requires Prophecy PhpUnit when using PHPUnit 9 or greater.","body":"#### Fix\n\n1. You must affect the $VENDOR_PATH dependencies, so navigate to the directory that controls them, e.g. `..\/$VENDOR_PATH`\n2. `composer require --dev phpspec\/prophecy-phpunit:^2`\n\n## Error: Class \"Drupal\\my_module\\Bar\" not found\n\n### Fix\n\n1. Use the `--flush` flag...\n2. `.\/run-phpunit-tests.sh --flush`\n\n* [Possible more info](https:\/\/drupal.stackexchange.com\/a\/299070)\n\n## Error in bootstrap script: ValueError:\n\nPath cannot be empty\n#0 \/Users\/aaronklump\/Code\/Projects\/MyProject\/site\/app\/web\/core\/tests\/Drupal\/TestTools\/PhpUnitCompatibility\/PhpUnit8\/ClassWriter.php(58): file_get_contents('')\n\n### Fix\n\nMake sure you have installed \"drupal\/core-dev\" at the same major\/minor as \"drupal\/core\"\n\n1. grep drupal\/core-recommended composer.json\n2. `composer require --dev drupal\/core-dev:`..., e.g. `composer require --dev drupal\/core-dev:9.5.11`\n\n## PHP Warning:  Undefined array key \"INSTALL_PATH\"\n\nHave you installed _drupal\/core-dev_ in your Drupal project?\n\n### Fix\n\n1. `composer require --dev drupal\/core-dev` in the Drupal root.\n\n## PHP Fatal error: Declaration of Symfony\\Component\\DependencyInjection\\ContainerInterface::has($id) must be compatible with Psr\\Container\\ContainerInterface::has(string $id): bool\n\n### Fix\n\nThis is probably happening on an older version of Drupal, where the container version is mismatched.\nChange the installed version of psr\/container to match that of the Drupal installation.\n\n```php\ncd tests_php_unit\ncomposer require psr\/container:^1.1\n```\n\n## Interface \"PHPUnit\\Runner\\BeforeFirstTestHook\" not found\n\n* Make sure you are installing\n* https:\/\/github.com\/sebastianbergmann\/phpunit\/issues\/4595"},{"id":"install","title":"Install","body":"## This Package\n\n```shell\nmkdir -p tests_phpunit\/src\necho \"\/vendor\/\\n*\/.cache\\n\/.phpunit.cache\\n\/reports\/\\n\" > tests_phpunit\/.gitignore\n```\n\n### Main Version\n\n```shell\necho '{\"autoload\":{\"psr-4\":{\"\\\\\\\\AKlump\\\\\\\\Drupal\\\\\\\\PHPUnit\\\\\\\\Integration\\\\\\\\\":\"src\"}},\"repositories\":[{\"type\":\"github\",\"url\":\"https:\/\/github.com\/aklump\/drupal-phpunit-integration\"}]}' > tests_phpunit\/composer.json\n(cd tests_phpunit && composer require aklump\/drupal-phpunit-integration:^10)\n```\n\n### Dev Version\n\n```shell\necho '{\"autoload\":{\"psr-4\":{\"\\\\\\\\AKlump\\\\\\\\Drupal\\\\\\\\PHPUnit\\\\\\\\Integration\\\\\\\\\":\"src\"}},\"repositories\":[{\"type\":\"path\",\"url\":\"\/Users\/aklump\/Code\/Packages\/php\/drupal-phpunit-integration\/app\"}]}' > tests_phpunit\/composer.json\n(cd tests_phpunit && composer require aklump\/drupal-phpunit-integration:@dev)\n```\n\n7.\n\n2. bin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh --flush"},{"id":"rewrite","title":"s-examples--3","body":"- locate in: my_module\/tests\/src\/Unit\/Double_DisplayManagerTest.php\n*  https:\/\/www.drupal.org\/docs\/develop\/automated-testing\/phpunit-in-drupal\/phpunit-file-structure-namespace-and-required-metadat```php\n\/**\n * @coversDefaultClass \\Drupal\\ice_cream\\IceCream\n * @group ice_cream\n *\/\nclass IceCreamTest extends UnitTestCase {\n  ...\n}\n```"},{"id":"conventional_vs_this_way","title":"The Conventional Way, Then This Package's Way","body":"[Why Do You Need This?](@why) makes the argument in prose. This page makes it in commands: the fully conventional way to run a Drupal module's tests with nothing but core itself, then exactly what this package changes (and doesn't) on top of it.\n\n## The conventional way\n\nThis is the entire contract Drupal core itself defines for Kernel and Functional tests. Nothing below is specific to any package, including this one.\n\n### 1. Install `drupal\/core-dev`\n\n```shell\ncomposer require --dev drupal\/core-dev:^10\n```\n\nThis is the one Composer dependency the convention needs. It provides `vendor\/bin\/phpunit` and every Drupal test base class (`KernelTestBase`, `BrowserTestBase`, `UnitTestCase`, \u2026).\n\n### 2. Write tests under the standard directories\n\n```\nweb\/modules\/custom\/my_module\/\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 tests\/\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 src\/\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500 Kernel\/\n        \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 FooTest.php        namespace Drupal\\Tests\\my_module\\Kernel\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500 Functional\/\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500 BarTest.php        namespace Drupal\\Tests\\my_module\\Functional\n```\n\nThat's the whole registration step. There is no `phpunit.xml` entry to add, no composer.json autoload-dev block for a test to be discovered. Core finds it because of where it lives and what its namespace says, not because anything was told to look for it.\n\n### 3. Export two environment variables, yourself, before running\n\n```shell\nexport SIMPLETEST_DB=\"mysql:\/\/user:pass@host:3306\/my_database\"\nexport SIMPLETEST_BASE_URL=\"http:\/\/my-site.local\/\"\n```\n\n### 4. Run core's own `phpunit.xml.dist` directly\n\n```shell\nvendor\/bin\/phpunit -c web\/core --testsuite=kernel,functional --group my_module\n```\n\n`-c web\/core` points PHPUnit at `core\/phpunit.xml.dist`, which is the actual, canonical configuration \u2014 the one drupal.org's own GitLab CI template uses under the hood, and the one every other Drupal developer's machine can run unmodified.\n\n### Why step 2 needs no registration: a look under the hood\n\n`core\/phpunit.xml.dist`'s `kernel` testsuite doesn't list directories \u2014 it delegates to a small PHP class:\n\n```xml\n\n  .\/tests\/TestSuites\/KernelTestSuite.php\n\n```\n\n```php\n\/\/ core\/tests\/TestSuites\/KernelTestSuite.php\npublic static function suite() {\n  $root = dirname(__DIR__, 3);\n  $suite = new static('kernel');\n  $suite->addTestsBySuiteNamespace($root, 'Kernel');\n  return $suite;\n}\n```\n\n`addTestsBySuiteNamespace()` walks the *entire* Drupal root \u2014 core, contrib, custom, all of it \u2014 looking for any `tests\/src\/Kernel` directory and adds every test it finds. `FunctionalTestSuite` does the identical thing for `tests\/src\/Functional`, and `UnitTestSuite` the identical thing for `tests\/src\/Unit`. This is why a module never registers its own tests anywhere: the convention is the directory and the namespace, nothing else. The exact discovery line, traced into `TestSuiteBase::addTestsBySuiteNamespace()`, is hardcoded:\n\n```php\n\/\/ core\/tests\/TestSuites\/TestSuiteBase.php\n$test_path = \"$dir\/tests\/src\/$suite_namespace\";\n```\n\nThere is no alternate path. A test file has to physically live at `\/tests\/src\/\/...` or core's discovery will never see it \u2014 not \"won't autoload it,\" won't even attempt to include it in the run. Autoloadable and discoverable are two different mechanisms, and this is the only one that decides what PHPUnit runs.\n\n(This particular discovery class is deprecated as of Drupal 10.3 and removed in 11.0 \u2014 PHPUnit 10 does test discovery differently \u2014 but the contract it implements, \"directory and namespace is the registration,\" is expected to survive the mechanism change. See https:\/\/www.drupal.org\/node\/3405829.)\n\n### What the conventional way *can't* see: a module's own `autoload-dev`\n\nThe directory convention above is the only registration that exists. `core\/tests\/bootstrap.php` is the actual code that wires up the autoloader for a test run, and it does this per extension, with nothing configurable:\n\n```php\n\/\/ core\/tests\/bootstrap.php, drupal_phpunit_get_extension_namespaces()\nif (is_dir($dir . '\/src')) {\n  $namespaces['Drupal\\\\' . $extension . '\\\\'][] = $dir . '\/src';\n}\nif (is_dir($dir . '\/tests\/src')) {\n  $namespaces['Drupal\\\\Tests\\\\' . $extension . '\\\\'][] = $dir . '\/tests\/src';\n}\n```\n\nThat's the entire autoloading contract for every extension, core or contrib: exactly two fixed namespaces, derived purely from two fixed directory names. This function never opens a module's `composer.json` \u2014 not its `autoload` key, not its `autoload-dev` key. If a module wants a shared test trait or helper class living somewhere other than `Drupal\\Tests\\\\` under `tests\/src\/` \u2014 say, a trait meant to be reused by *other* modules' tests, declared via its own `autoload-dev` \u2014 the conventional path has no mechanism to find it, full stop.\n\nIt's not only that bootstrap.php declines to look: Composer itself wouldn't honor that declaration either way. Composer's own schema documentation marks the `autoload-dev` key `[root-only]` \u2014 it is only ever processed for the project's own root `composer.json`, and is explicitly ignored in every dependency package, your custom module included. Two independent reasons, same result: a module-level `autoload-dev` is invisible to the conventional path.\n\nThis package's `AutoloadDev` class exists specifically to undo that: for each directory your `phpunit.xml` lists in a ``, it walks up to the nearest `composer.json` and merges that package's own `autoload-dev` into the cached PSR-4 map \u2014 the one case in this whole comparison where this package does something the conventional way has no equivalent for at all, rather than just automating it. See [Composer Autoload Dev](@composer_autoload_dev).\n\n## Proof this really is the entire contract\n\nThe exact test files this package would normally run for `auto_retina` were pointed at core's own `phpunit.xml.dist`, with the two variables above exported by hand and this package nowhere in the invocation or the module's own `composer.json`:\n\n```shell\nexport SIMPLETEST_DB=\"mysql:\/\/drupal10:drupal10@database:3306\/drupal10\"\nexport SIMPLETEST_BASE_URL=\"http:\/\/drupal10.local.loft\/\"\nvendor\/bin\/phpunit -c web\/core --testsuite=kernel,functional --group auto_retina --testdox\n```\n\n```\nOK (18 tests, 39 assertions)\n```\n\nNo module-specific configuration beyond those two lines. That's the baseline this package has to earn its place against.\n\n## Where the conventional way costs you, every day\n\n1. **Discovery re-scans the whole codebase on every invocation.** There is no cache; core can't assume what changed since last time, so it doesn't try.\n2. **You personally re-derive two values, every session, on every machine.** Not because core is wrong to ask for them \u2014 it has no way to know your local database or site URL \u2014 but because nothing computes them for you.\n\n## This package's way: the same contract, made fast and automatic\n\nEverything in \"The conventional way\" above is still exactly true when this package is installed. Nothing about writing a Kernel or Functional test changes. What changes is who pays the two costs above, and when:\n\n| | Conventional | This package |\n|---|---|---|\n| Test discovery | Full codebase scan, every run | Scanned once, cached to a file, rescanned only on `--flush` (`BootstrapCache`) |\n| `SIMPLETEST_DB` | You export it, by hand, every session | Resolved automatically \u2014 your own exported value first, then your git branch's own `DATABASE_URL`, then plain `DATABASE_URL`, then Lando, then `drush sql:connect` (`DynamicConfig`) |\n| `SIMPLETEST_BASE_URL` | You export it, by hand, every session | Resolved automatically \u2014 your own value first, then Lando, then Drush's site URI (`DynamicConfig`) |\n| How the values reach a Kernel\/Functional test | A real process environment variable (`export`), inherited by the subprocess PHPUnit forks for every test | `putenv()` \u2014 a real process environment variable, for the same reason: see [Why Do You Need This?](@why) on `runTestInSeparateProcess` |\n| Command | `vendor\/bin\/phpunit -c web\/core --testsuite=kernel,functional --group my_module` | `bin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh` |\n| Test discovery registration (Kernel\/Functional) | None \u2014 directory and namespace only | None \u2014 directory and namespace only, unchanged |\n| A module's own `autoload-dev` namespaces | **Never loaded.** `bootstrap.php` only wires up two fixed namespaces by directory name; it never reads any module's `composer.json`. Composer itself wouldn't honor it either way \u2014 `autoload-dev` is root-only. | Scanned and merged into the cached PSR-4 map automatically (`AutoloadDev`, rebuilt on `--flush`) \u2014 no equivalent to \"automate,\" since none exists conventionally |\n\nThe \"Test discovery registration\" row is the one that matters most for trusting this package: for Kernel and Functional tests, it adds **zero** new registration steps on top of the conventional ones. A module's Kernel\/Functional tests written against this package are the same tests, in the same place, with the same namespace, that core's own `phpunit.xml.dist` would find on its own.\n\nThe `autoload-dev` row is the one exception to that pattern \u2014 it isn't automating an existing conventional step, because no such step exists. That's also true of one more thing the table above doesn't attempt to capture: **Unit** and **Integration** tests that use Drupal classes with no bootstrap at all. Neither of those two is \"the same contract, made faster\" \u2014 there is no core contract for either one to accelerate. See [Why Do You Need This?](@why) for that half of the story.\n\n## A fixed foot-gun: Unit tests now live where core already looks\n\nCore's \"Unit\" discovery works exactly like Kernel and Functional, down to the same hardcoded line: `$test_path = \"$dir\/tests\/src\/Unit\"`. A plain `PHPUnit\\Framework\\TestCase` with zero Drupal dependency, placed at `tests\/src\/Unit\/FooTest.php` with namespace `Drupal\\Tests\\my_module\\Unit`, is already discovered and run by core's own `phpunit.xml.dist`, with no bootstrap, no database, and no package of any kind installed. Core already has exactly the \"boots nothing\" Unit test type this package also offers.\n\nThis package's documentation used to place that same test type at `tests\/Unit\/FooTest.php` instead \u2014 one directory shallower, missing the `src\/` segment core's discovery requires. That one missing path segment was enough to make the file permanently invisible to `vendor\/bin\/phpunit -c web\/core`, `core\/scripts\/run-tests.sh`, and drupal.org's GitLab CI template, for a test type that had no technical reason to diverge from the conventional path at all. It was never autoloading that broke \u2014 `AutoloadDev` or a plain `autoload-dev` entry can make the class loadable either way \u2014 it was discovery, which checks the literal directory string and nothing else.\n\nThat's fixed now: both flavors live under `tests\/src\/`, the same root core itself uses:\n\n```\nweb\/modules\/custom\/alpha\/tests\/src\/Unit\/FooTest.php\nweb\/modules\/custom\/alpha\/tests\/src\/Integration\/FooTest.php\n```\n\n**Integration tests remain a different story, and were never a flaw in the same sense.** \"Integration\" is not a suite core defines at all \u2014 `UnitTestSuite`, `KernelTestSuite`, and `FunctionalTestSuite` are the only three for an extension's own tests, with no fourth for a no-bootstrap-but-uses-Drupal-classes flavor. Since there is no conventional \"integration\" suite to fall back to, `tests\/src\/Integration\/` costs nothing `tests\/Integration\/` didn't already lack \u2014 drupal.org's CI template was never going to run an \"integration\" suite either way, directory or no directory. It moved to `tests\/src\/` anyway, for one consistent rule rather than two.\n\n## A test-only dependency, and the two different \"root\"s that matter\n\nFixing the directory only solves *discovery*. A Unit or Integration test that needs a third-party class \u2014 Mockery, Faker, anything beyond Drupal core itself \u2014 has a second, separate question: *which* `composer.json` actually has to declare it for the class to be installed anywhere at all.\n\nA contrib module has no site of its own, so \"add it to the site's root `composer.json`\" \u2014 the obvious-looking answer by analogy with everything else in this comparison \u2014 doesn't even apply: there is no root the module's author controls. The real answer turns out to depend on which of two genuinely different \"conventional\" paths is running the test, and they disagree.\n\n**drupal.org's actual GitLab CI already solves this, by merging your module's manifest into its own.** The template's `scripts\/expand_composer_json.php` builds the scaffold site it tests your module against by deep-merging *your module's own* `composer.json` \u2014 `require`, `require-dev`, everything \u2014 into the generated manifest, before running `composer install` against the result. A dependency you declare in your own module's `require-dev` is not a dependency of some separate root the CI owns; it becomes part of the root CI builds, by design. For drupal.org's real pipeline, your module's own `require-dev` is exactly the right, fully-supported place for a test-only package. (Verified against the live script at `git.drupalcode.org\/project\/gitlab_templates`, not inferred from Composer's general root-only rule \u2014 that rule is real, but this template works around it deliberately.)\n\n**This package's own local runner does not do the same merge, and that's the actual gap.** `bin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh` runs `tests_phpunit\/vendor\/bin\/phpunit`, against `tests_phpunit\/`'s own, separate Composer project. `AutoloadDev` merges a module's `autoload-dev` *namespace map* into that project's autoloader, but nothing merges the module's `require-dev` *packages* into `tests_phpunit\/composer.json` the way `expand_composer_json.php` does for the scaffold. A package declared only in your module's own `require-dev` is therefore exactly backwards from what the directory-convention fix addressed: it's discoverable and correctly placed, installed and working under drupal.org's real CI, and yet absent locally under this package's own runner \u2014 the opposite failure direction, and arguably the more dangerous one, since it means a maintainer can see green in CI and \"class not found\" on their own machine, or the reverse, depending on which vendor happens to hold the class that day.\n\n**The fix is a code change, not a rule, and it isn't built yet.** Making local and CI agree means this package's own bootstrap should do what `expand_composer_json.php` already proves works: read the tested module's own `require-dev`, and actually install those packages into (or alongside) `tests_phpunit\/vendor`, not just map their namespaces. Until that exists, a module-level `require-dev` test dependency is genuinely unsafe to rely on with this package's local runner, even though it's the correct and fully portable choice for drupal.org's own CI.\n\n## What this means for you\n\n- Nothing about how you *write* a Kernel or Functional test changes. The same file runs, unmodified, through `vendor\/bin\/phpunit -c web\/core` with hand-exported variables, through `bin\/run-phpunit-tests.sh` with this package installed, and through drupal.org's GitLab CI template.\n- A Unit test written at `tests\/src\/Unit\/` gets the same dual portability for free, as long as it doesn't lean on a third-party dependency \u2014 if it does, today, only drupal.org's real CI is guaranteed to have it; this package's local runner is not, until it gains its own equivalent of `expand_composer_json.php`.\n- An Integration test has no conventional path to fall back to either way, so its directory choice doesn't cost portability it could otherwise have had.\n- If this package were deleted from a project tonight, every Kernel, Functional, and Unit test would still run tomorrow \u2014 slower, and only after you export two variables by hand again \u2014 provided none of them depend on a third-party package only this package's own `tests_phpunit\/` ever installed. That's the whole bet this package makes: it should always be safe to fall back to the row on the left.\n\n## Related topics\n\n- [Why Do You Need This?](@why)\n- [Composer Autoload Dev](@composer_autoload_dev)"},{"id":"why","title":"Why Do You Need This?","body":"* https:\/\/www.drupal.org\/docs\/develop\/automated-testing\n* https:\/\/www.drupal.org\/docs\/develop\/automated-testing\/phpunit-in-drupal\n\nThis package does two different things. Neither one changes what a \"correct\" Drupal test is, or invents a competing way to write one \u2014 see [The Conventional Way, Then This Package's Way](@conventional_vs_this_way) for a line-by-line comparison. This page is about *why* each half exists.\n\n## 1. It fills a real gap: tests that use Drupal classes but need no bootstrap at all\n\nDrupal's own test types sit at two extremes. `Drupal\\Tests\\UnitTestCase` still pulls in Drupal's test base classes and container plumbing, even for a test that doesn't touch the database. Kernel and functional tests need a real database and a bootstrapped site. A large amount of ordinary module code falls in the gap between those two: it type-hints Drupal interfaces (`EntityInterface`, `ConfigFactoryInterface`, \u2026) and needs real Drupal classes to mock against, but its own logic is pure PHP \u2014 no query, no request, no filesystem.\n\nCore gives that code nowhere good to live \u2014 and this isn't a matter of degree, it's absolute. `core\/tests\/bootstrap.php` wires up the test autoloader with exactly two fixed, hardcoded namespaces per extension, derived purely from directory names (`Drupal\\\\` from `src\/`, `Drupal\\Tests\\\\` from `tests\/src\/`); it never opens any module's `composer.json`, so a module's own `autoload-dev` \u2014 a shared test trait, say, meant for other modules to reuse \u2014 is invisible to it, full stop. Composer itself wouldn't honor that declaration either way: its schema docs mark `autoload-dev` `[root-only]`, meaning it's only ever processed for the root project, never a dependency. Two independent reasons, same dead end.\n\nThis package does give that code somewhere to live, with two test flavors that need nothing but plain PHPUnit and an autoloader:\n\n- **Unit** tests have zero Drupal dependency \u2014 no `UnitTestCase`, no bootstrap, no autoload-dev scanning. They run as fast as any PHP test ever runs.\n- **Integration** tests may use real Drupal classes (and this package's `MockDrupalEntityTrait` for realistic entity mocks), loaded from a namespace map this package builds once and caches by actually reading each module's own `autoload-dev` \u2014 the one thing neither Composer nor core's bootstrap does. See [Composer Autoload Dev](@composer_autoload_dev) for how.\n\nNeither flavor is a Drupal convention at all \u2014 core has no name for \"a test that uses Drupal classes but boots nothing.\" This package invented one because the gap is real and common, not because core's own test types were insufficient at what *they* do. See [The Conventional Way, Then This Package's Way](@conventional_vs_this_way) for the two-independent-reasons case traced through actual source.\n\n## 2. For the tests that do need Drupal's database (Kernel, Functional), it removes toil without changing the contract\n\nThis half is different in kind from the first: Kernel and Functional tests already have a fully conventional, fully sufficient way to run, defined by Drupal core itself \u2014 `core\/phpunit.xml.dist`, two environment variables (`SIMPLETEST_DB`, `SIMPLETEST_BASE_URL`), and `drupal\/core-dev`. This package does not replace, extend, or reinterpret that contract. It automates two specific pieces of manual toil in running it, confirmed by tracing core's own source and then proving the result against a real module's real tests:\n\n**Test discovery rescans the whole codebase, every single invocation.** Core's own `KernelTestSuite`\/`FunctionalTestSuite` classes call `addTestsBySuiteNamespace()`, which walks the entire Drupal root looking for every module's `tests\/src\/Kernel` or `tests\/src\/Functional` directory \u2014 on every `phpunit` run, with no caching, by design (it has to, since it doesn't know what changed). For a tight local edit-test loop, paying that scan on every single run is pure overhead. `BootstrapCache` caches the discovered namespace map to a file and only rebuilds it when you pass `--flush`.\n\n**`SIMPLETEST_DB`\/`SIMPLETEST_BASE_URL` are not static values you set once.** They differ per developer machine, per Lando app, and sometimes per git branch if you keep more than one database fixture around. Nobody enjoys re-deriving a `mysql:\/\/` connection string or looking up today's Lando URL by hand before every test run, and \"I forgot to export `SIMPLETEST_DB`\" is one of the most frequently asked Drupal testing questions on record for exactly that reason. `DynamicConfig` resolves both automatically \u2014 your own exported value first, then your git branch's own `DATABASE_URL`, then a plain `DATABASE_URL`, then Lando, then `drush sql:connect` \u2014 and fails loudly with exact next steps when none of those produce a value, instead of letting the test suite blanket-fail with no explanation.\n\n### Proof, not assertion\n\nThe second half above isn't a hunch \u2014 the same test files this package would normally run were pointed directly at Drupal core's own `phpunit.xml.dist`, with both environment variables exported by hand, and no trace of this package anywhere in the invocation:\n\n```shell\nexport SIMPLETEST_DB=\"mysql:\/\/drupal10:drupal10@database:3306\/drupal10\"\nexport SIMPLETEST_BASE_URL=\"http:\/\/drupal10.local.loft\/\"\nvendor\/bin\/phpunit -c web\/core --testsuite=kernel,functional --group auto_retina --testdox\n```\n\n```\nOK (18 tests, 39 assertions)\n```\n\nEighteen tests, zero module-specific configuration beyond the two variables above, and zero involvement from this package \u2014 which is exactly the point: the tests this package runs for you are never special. It is free to disappear from the picture entirely, any time, with the same tests still passing the same way. What it buys you is not having to re-derive those two values and re-pay that discovery scan every time you want to run them.\n\n## The one rule that keeps this honest\n\nBoth of `DynamicConfig`'s values are set with a real `putenv()` call, not just a `$_ENV[...] = ...` assignment \u2014 and that distinction is not cosmetic. `KernelTestBase` and `BrowserTestBase` both declare `protected $runTestInSeparateProcess = TRUE;` in core itself, so PHPUnit forks a brand-new PHP process for *every single test method*. A plain `$_ENV` assignment lives only in the parent process's memory and never reaches that child; a real process environment variable, set via `putenv()`, is inherited across the fork the same way any shell `export` would be. Get this wrong \u2014 as this package's own `0.0.x` line did \u2014 and every Kernel\/Functional test fails identically with a confusing \"no database connection\" or \"no base URL\" error that has nothing apparent to do with the test itself.\n\nThe underlying design rule, worth restating for anyone extending this package: it must only ever compute the same value a developer would conventionally `export` by hand, and must deliver it the same way a real export would \u2014 never a parallel, package-specific mechanism that only works when this package is present. The day it stops doing that, it stops being \"the same tests, faster\" and becomes a second, incompatible way to test Drupal \u2014 which is the one thing it must never become.\n\n## Related topics\n\n- [The Conventional Way, Then This Package's Way](@conventional_vs_this_way)\n- [Composer Autoload Dev](@composer_autoload_dev)\n- [Install](@install)"}]